      "classification": "different_products_origins_or_programs",
      "reason": "Claim 1 states Indonesia's nickel export ban had no direct impact on US production/importation, while Claim 2 describes a specific US-Indonesia trade deal maintaining a 19% reciprocal tariff on imports. These address different mechanisms (export ban impact vs. bilateral tariff rate) and do not contradict; one addresses supply chain volume effects, the other specific duty rates.",
      "verification_question": "Does the 19% reciprocal tariff apply to all Indonesian nickel imports or only specific categories excluded from the export ban discussion?"
    },
    "conflict_d0bd82afa8f48c6a4b1dad3d": {
      "classification": "different_dates_or_policy_stages",
      "reason": "Claim 1 describes a ratified deal to eliminate tariffs on hundreds of products, while Claim 2 describes a new framework effective July 25 maintaining a 10% baseline with exemptions. The text in Claim 2 explicitly states the Trump administration 'replaced the temporary 10% global tariff' with this revised structure, indicating sequential policy stages rather than a direct conflict.",
      "verification_question": "What is the specific effective date of the tariff elimination deal versus the July 25 baseline framework implementation?"
    },
    "conflict_6116b9dc0125aac247258be1": {
      "classification": "different_dates_or_policy_stages",
      "reason": "Claim 1 states the US will eliminate reciprocal tariffs imposed earlier in the year, while Claim 2 describes a new framework effective July 25 maintaining a 10% baseline. The text in Claim 2 clarifies that the Trump administration replaced a temporary tariff with this revised structure, indicating a policy evolution where elimination of specific earlier tariffs coexists with a new baseline.",
      "verification_question": "Are the 'reciprocal tariffs imposed earlier this year' distinct from the 'temporary 10% global tariff' mentioned in the July 25 framework?"
    },
    "conflict_fdfd98b5dd3104e9916231cc": {
      "classification": "complementary_or_no_conflict",
      "reason": "Claim 1 states Section 301 action raised EV tariffs to 100% from Jan 1, 2025, while Claim 2 states the same action kept Lists 1-4A at 7.5-25%. The text explicitly notes that Lists 1-4A remained at the lower rate while 'several categories sharply' raised to higher rates (EVs, semiconductors, batteries), indicating a general rule with specific exceptions rather than a contradiction.",
      "verification_question": "Do electric vehicles fall under the specific categories raised to 100% or are they included in the Lists 1-4A range of 7.5-25%?"
    },
    "conflict_b79c96034f376fb25a827b8d": {
      "classification": "different_products_origins_or_programs",
      "reason": "Claim 1 states Trump applied 25% tariffs on certain non-compliant imports from Canada and Mexico, while Claim 2 states a proclamation imposed 25% tariffs on steel from every country except Canada and Mexico. These address different scopes: one targets specific non-compliant goods (potentially autos/parts) based on trade agreement compliance, the other targets all steel with explicit origin exclusions.",
      "verification_question": "Does the 'certain imports' in Claim 1 refer to steel or other products like automobiles, and does the exemption in Claim 2 apply to all Canadian/Mexican steel regardless of compliance status?"
    }
  }
